useful information by measuring things such as power consumption,
function execution time, and even electromagnetic radiation. A sub-
set of these attacks that are performed by monitoring different side
channels is known as passive attacks. On the other hand, there are
active hardware attacks that are carried out by manipulating inputs
such as power and clock or changing environmental conditions such
as increasing temperature; which are known as fault injection attacks.
In these active attacks, the attacker generally tries to inject faults and
change the correct functioning of the system. A successful attack can
have different goals depending on the type of attack, so in attacks based
on side-channel analysis, a successful attack is to obtain important
information, but in attacks based on fault injection, a successful attack
is to disrupt the correct process of the system.
With the emergence of hardware attacks, great challenges have been
created for embedded systems, which allow the attacker to break all
kinds of protections and encryptions done at the software level [5].
Therefore, this type of attacks can have residual risks for embedded
systems that are sometimes used in critical safety applications. One
of the primary measures to prevent these attacks is to evaluate them
against various types of hardware attacks before entering the market,
but in some business approaches, short time to market takes priority
over security assessments, and in most cases, vulnerabilities based on
the hardware is not fully examined when entering the market [4].
Therefore, an embedded system may be vulnerable to this type of attack
even after entering the market. Hence, before using these systems, it
is essential that the developers of embedded systems evaluate them
against hardware attacks.
The evaluation of vulnerabilities based on hardware attack can be
done using different methods, which can be divided into 3 general
categories: (A) Techniques based on mathematical analysis, (B) using
simulation tools, and (C) experimental analysis techniques. The first
two groups use mathematical techniques and simulation software to
assess the vulnerability of a system against hardware attacks that have
wide capabilities at low cost. But these approaches are only suitable for
initial evaluation, because in the field of hardware-based attacks, vari-
ous environmental parameters such as process variation, temperature,
etc., play a role, which are not considered in these two techniques. As
a result, any arbitrary attack cannot be described and evaluated in the
environment of simulation or mathematical analysis [6,7].
The third group uses experimental analysis methods to evaluate
hardware security and investigate possible risks in a real environ-
ment [8]. The main challenge of this method is the need for a fault
generation and evaluation platform, which will be difficult for users
who do not have expertise in security and hardware evaluation. Until
now, various fault injection techniques have been proposed; among
these methods, attacks based on fault injection on the clock have
attracted the attention of many attackers due to their low cost and no
need for expensive equipment. Therefore, this type of attack can be a
serious risk for users of embedded systems. On the other hand, it will
be difficult and time-consuming to assess the vulnerability of embedded
systems against fault injection attacks that are based on clock signals.
In this Paper, by providing a vulnerability assessment platform
for embedded software against fault injection attacks that are carried
out on the clock signal, we will try to give this possibility to their
users in a short time, and with moderate expertise, they can find the
vulnerabilities of their own embedded software programs. Our main
aim is to practically investigate vulnerabilities related to embedded
software programs running on a processor based on RISC-V micro
architecture. To this end, our contributions in this work are as follows:
•An overview of hardware attacks, especially attacks based on
clock signal manipulation: At the first step, we provide a general
overview of the basic concepts of hardware attacks and their
types. More details about clock-based attacks, which are in line
with the main topic of this work, are provided. The reason for this
focus is the reasonable cost and availability of facilities needed for
our experiments.
•Overview of fault generators on the clock signal: In order to per-
form clock-based attacks in practice, it is necessary to implement
a fault generator. For this purpose, all the proposed methods for
fault generators in the clock signal are reviewed and compared
in the second part. Collecting the previous works will help us to
implement the suitable fault generator hardware circuit.
•Providing a fault generation platform: improving the pre-introd-
uced fault generators and providing a fault injection platform that
is able to generate different types of faults and then inject them
on the clock signal.
•Implementation of a platform to collect details of successful at-
tacks: The proposed fault generation platform generates and in-
jects all of the different types of faults. Therefore, we will try
to save all the information of the experimental tests by provid-
ing and implementing a unit’s hardware in order to collect the
characteristics of successful attacks.
•Analysis of the results and suggestions for its improvement: By an-
alyzing and examining successful attacks and their characteristics,
we try to find the vulnerability window of embedded software
programs.
The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews the
basic concepts and previous state of the art techniques. In Section 3, we
will first present our fault injection platform, then we will implement
the RISC-V based processor and finally we will explain the informa-
tion collection platform. Section 4 analyzes and reviews the obtained
results, and finally, the conclusion and future work are presented in
Section 5.

